      "message": "Gemini feedback:\n\n----\n\nExtracting magic values into named constants turns implicit knowledge into explicit documentation. It tells the next developer reading your tests exactly *why* a value was chosen, rather than forcing them to guess if `5` or `0xAA` holds special protocol significance.\n\nHere are the specific changes to eliminate the magic values across the test suite.\n\n### **1. Arbitrary Read Sizes and Bytes**\n\n**Location:** `util/focaltech/usb_device_test.cc` -\u003e `RecvForwardsToTransport`\n\n**Rationale:** Hardcoding `5` and `0xAB` obscures the intent of the test. The test is verifying that a partial read from the hardware correctly bubbles up to the caller. By explicitly naming these `kPartialReadSize` and `kArbitraryDataByte`, you signal to the reader that the exact numbers don\u0027t matter—only the consistency between the mocked return and the final assertion does.\n\n**Changes Needed:**\nDefine the constants near the top of the test file (inside the anonymous namespace):\n\n```cpp\nconstexpr size_t kPartialReadSize \u003d 5;\nconstexpr uint8_t kArbitraryDataByte \u003d 0xAB;\n\n```\n\nUpdate the test logic (incorporating the `std::ranges` and trailing-return-type cleanups we discussed earlier):\n\n```cpp\n  // Inside RecvForwardsToTransport:\n  EXPECT_CALL(*mock_, Recv(_, timeout))\n      .WillOnce(Invoke([\u0026](std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e data, auto) {\n        std::ranges::fill(data.first(kPartialReadSize), kArbitraryDataByte);\n        return kPartialReadSize;\n      }));\n\n  auto result \u003d device_-\u003eRecv(buffer, timeout);\n  ASSERT_TRUE(result.has_value());\n  EXPECT_EQ(*result, kPartialReadSize);\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **2. Invalid Signatures and Magic Numbers**\n\n**Location:** `util/focaltech/updater_test.cc` and `util/focaltech/ft_scsi_test.cc`\n\n**Rationale:** `0xDEADBEEF` is universally recognized as garbage memory, but in tests designed to verify how the system handles *corruption*, it is better to name it exactly what it represents in that context. This makes the struct initialization instantly readable.\n\n**Changes Needed:**\nIn `updater_test.cc` (inside the anonymous namespace):\n\n```cpp\nconstexpr uint32_t kInvalidMagicNumber \u003d 0xDEADBEEF;\n\n// Inside UpdateFw_FailsOnInvalidMagicNumber:\nConfigPage config{.code_valid_control_word \u003d kInvalidMagicNumber};\n\n```\n\nIn `ft_scsi_test.cc` (inside the anonymous namespace):\n\n```cpp\nconstexpr uint32_t kInvalidCswSignature \u003d 0xDEADBEEF;\n\n// Inside SendBulkDataOut_CswSignatureMismatch:\n  BulkCsWrap csw{\n      .signature \u003d ToLittleEndian\u003cuint32_t\u003e(kInvalidCswSignature),\n      .tag \u003d ToLittleEndian\u003cuint32_t\u003e(1),\n      .data_residue \u003d 0,\n      .status \u003d CswStatus::kCommandPassed,\n  };\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### **3. Arbitrary Payload Bytes**\n\n**Location:** `util/focaltech/test_utils.h` and various test files\n\n**Rationale:** There are multiple places where `0xAA` or `0xAB` are hardcoded to fill max-size buffers just to simulate outbound or inbound data. Defining a single shared constant in your test utilities ensures uniformity and clearly marks the data as placeholder payload.\n\n**Changes Needed:**\nAdd the constant to `util/focaltech/test_utils.h`:\n\n```cpp\nnamespace focaltech::testing {\n\n// ... existing code ...\n\ninline constexpr uint8_t kArbitraryPayloadByte \u003d 0xAA;\n\n}  // namespace focaltech::testing\n\n```\n\nUpdate the vector initializations in `ft_scsi_test.cc`:\n\n```cpp\n// Inside SendBulkDataOut_DataOutSuccess:\nstd::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e data_buffer(kUsbBulkMaxPacketSize, focaltech::testing::kArbitraryPayloadByte);\n\n// Inside ReceiveBulkDataIn_Success:\n      .WillOnce(Invoke(\n          [](std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e data, auto) {\n            std::ranges::fill(data, focaltech::testing::kArbitraryPayloadByte);\n            return kUsbBulkMaxPacketSize;\n          }))\n\n```\n\nUpdate the vector initialization in `updater_test.cc` (`UpdateFw_FailsOnMissingConfigPage`):\n\n```cpp\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e fw_data(sizeof(ConfigPage) + kFocalConfigSize,\n                               focaltech::testing::kArbitraryPayloadByte);\n\n```",
