      "side": 1,
      "message": "Now that we\u0027re using designated initializers, this is redundant and we can just use designated initializers directly everywhere.\n\n----\n\n\n**Location:** `util/focaltech/ft_scsi.cc` and `updater.cc`\n\n**The Issue:** The commit message mentions standardizing command creation using the new `BuildFtCmd` helper. However, the author seems to have discovered C++20 designated initializers halfway through writing the patch. As a result, `updater.cc` uses beautiful designated initializers almost everywhere, but `GetBootVersion` and the test files still use `BuildFtCmd`.\n\n**Why it matters:** `BuildFtCmd` requires passing up to six positional arguments, which often includes \"magic zeros\" (e.g., `BuildFtCmd(Opcode::kReadInfo, Subcmd::kReadData, 0, 0, 0, 0)`). Designated initializers (`CmdFormat{ .opcode \u003d ... }`) are self-documenting and strictly superior for readability.\n\n**Remediation:** 1\\. Delete the `BuildFtCmd` function entirely from `ft_scsi.h` and `ft_scsi.cc`. 2\\. Update the remaining calls to use designated initializers. For example, in `GetBootVersion`:\n\n```c\n// Old:\nconst auto cmd \u003d BuildFtCmd(Opcode::kReadInfo, Subcmd::kReadData, 0, 0, 0, 0);\n\n// New:\nconst CmdFormat cmd{\n    .opcode \u003d Opcode::kReadInfo,\n    .subcmd \u003d Subcmd::kReadData,\n}; // Omitted fields are automatically zero-initialized\n\n```",
