      "side": 1,
      "message": "**Location:** `util/focaltech/ft_scsi_test.cc` and `updater_test.cc`\n\n**The Issue:** Across both test files, the exact same lambda is written over a dozen times to simulate a valid USB CSW response:\n\n```c\n  .WillOnce(Invoke([](std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e data, auto) {\n      testing::BuildValidCsw(data, 1);\n      return testing::kUsbCswSize;\n  }))\n\n```\n\n**Remediation:** GoogleMock provides the `ACTION` macro specifically to DRY up this kind of repetitive side-effect logic. You can define a custom action at the top of your test file (or in `test_utils.h`) and vastly simplify your test cases.\n\n```c\n// Define this once:\nACTION_P(ReturnValidCsw, tag) {\n  // arg0 corresponds to the first argument of Recv (std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e data)\n  focaltech::testing::BuildValidCsw(arg0, tag);\n  return focaltech::testing::kUsbCswSize;\n}\n\n// And then replace all the verbose lambdas with this clean one-liner:\nEXPECT_CALL(*mock_, Recv(_, _)).WillOnce(ReturnValidCsw(1));\n\n```",
